## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical iOS fundamentals: Codable data models, file-based persistence with atomic writes, and SwiftUI data flow. It also exercises UX concerns like debounce for responsiveness and accessibility considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Codable data model for Note with title, body, tags, and date
- File I/O in Documents with atomic write pattern
- SwiftUI state management using @Published and binding for filters
- Combine debouncing for search input
- Tag-based filtering logic and accessibility labeling

## Code Example
```javascript
import Foundation

struct Note: Codable, Identifiable {
  var id: UUID = UUID()
  var title: String
  var body: String
  var tags: [String]
  var date: Date = Date()
}

final class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var notes: [Note] = []
  private let fileURL: URL = FileManager.default
    .urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
    .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")

  init() { load() }

  func load() {
    guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return }
    if let loaded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data) {
      notes = loaded
    }
  }

  func save() {
    let tempURL = fileURL.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
    guard let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(notes) else { return }
    try? data.write(to: tempURL)
    try? FileManager.default.replaceItemAtURL(fileURL, withItemAtURL: tempURL)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to thousands of notes while keeping memory usage low?
- How would you test atomic write correctness and debounce behavior?
- How would you migrate to Core Data or add cloud sync later?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical iOS development skills, including Core Data modeling, SwiftUI data binding, secure encryption with CryptoKit, and fundamental testing practices.

## Key Concepts
- Core Data entity modeling and persistence
- SwiftUI reactive data flow with `@FetchRequest`
- Codable JSON serialization and file I/O
- CryptoKit AES-GCM symmetric encryption
- Secure storage for encryption keys (Keychain)
- Unit testing for persistence, encoding, and encryption

## Code Example
```swift
struct Entry: Codable {
    let id: UUID
    let timestamp: Date
    let mood: Int16
    let text: String
}

class EntryStore: ObservableObject {
    @Published var entries: [Entry] = []

    func exportToEncryptedJSON(key: SymmetricKey) throws -> URL {
        let encoder = JSONEncoder()
        encoder.dateEncodingStrategy = .iso8601
        let data = try encoder.encode(entries)
        let sealedBox = try AES.GCM.seal(data, using: key)
        let encryptedData = sealedBox.combined!
        let url = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
            .appendingPathComponent("mood_export.enc")
        try encryptedData.write(to: url)
        return url
    }
}
```

